 ## Overview
-The Scanned AOM Holographic Display is a time-multiplexed volumetric display technique that generates large apparent holographic fringes by rapidly scanning a phase-modulated light beam across the viewer's eye [S1]. It solves the hardware limitation of Acousto-Optic Modulators (AOMs), which can only phase-modulate a small number of samples (approximately 1000) at any single instant due to the finite bandwidth and aperture of the acoustic wave [S1]. Unlike static SLM-based methods, this approach synthesizes a large hologram by exploiting the human visual system's integration time (typically 20 ms) to merge the scanned, time-multiplexed samples into a coherent 3D image [S1].
+The Scanned AOM Holographic Display is a time-multiplexed volumetric display technique that generates large apparent holographic fringes by rapidly scanning a phase-modulated light beam across the viewer's eye [S1]. It solves the hardware limitation of Acousto-Optic Modulators (AOMs), which can only phase-modulate a small number of samples (approximately 1000) at any single instant due to the finite bandwidth and aperture of the acoustic wave [S1]. Unlike static SLM-based methods, this approach synthesizes a large hologram by exploiting the human visual system's integration time (typically 20 ms) to merge the scanned, time-multiplexed samples into a coherent 3D image [S1]. It builds on [Holographic Computer-Generated Holography (CGH) Calculation](6884-holographic-computer-generated-holography-cgh-calculation.md).
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A set of computed holographic fringes (complex amplitude data) representing the scene.
-*   **Output:** A real-time 3D holographic image visible to the observer.
